# Local machine configuration, never published
.claude/
.tools/

# The private-term list for the leak scanner. A list of things that must not be
# published is itself a list of private things, so it never enters the repo.
# tools/private-terms.example.json shows the shape.
.private-terms.json

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
*.key
*.pem
secret-*.txt
credentials*.json

# Gate artifacts
*.gate-backup
.*.gate-staged

# Build noise
node_modules/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.log

# Audit scratch space. Red-team runs create victim documents here, and those
# fixtures imitate real client material on purpose. They must never be published.
.attack-lab/

# The whole docs/ folder is INTERNAL. It holds the working plan, the session
# diary, the decision log and the pending list: written in Portuguese, naming the
# author and their employer, recording decisions that are ours rather than the
# project's. It lives here so it stays next to the code between sessions, but none
# of it is part of what gets published.
#
# Ignoring the folder rather than each file on purpose: listing them one by one
# means the next internal note someone adds is public by default, which is the
# wrong direction for a default to fail in.
docs/
